Conversely, if $f : X \to Y$ is a dominant morphism of varieties, $X$ is Cohen-Macaulay, $Y$ is nonsingular, and all fibres have the same dimension $r$, then $f$ is flat. This follows from Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00R4" data-tag="00R4">10.128.1</a> and Varieties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0B2L" data-tag="0B2L">33.20.4</a> showing $\dim (X) = \dim (Y) + r$.